er):\n\n| Candidate | Product 0 | Product 1 | Order fees / storage | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 0 | 0 | — | **0** |\n| 4 | −320 | −450 | — | about −770 |\n| 7 | — | — | saves ~320 in fees, ~530 extra storage | about −700 to −900 |\n| 1 | −350 | −610 | — | about −960 |\n| 6 | — | — | saves ~460 in fees, ~1,100 extra storage | about −1,500 |\n| 3 | 0 | −1,800 | — | about −1,800 |\n| 2 | −730 | −2,270 | — | about −3,000 |\n| 5 | −1,640 | −1,820 | — | about −3,460 |\n\nFor candidates 6 and 7 I didn't estimate the products separately. Their totals assume they behave like daily ordering at about 1.05×, then add the fee savings and extra storage costs.\n\n- **Candidate 6:** product 0 can't build a buffer from one batch to the next, because leftovers spoil the evening the next batch arrives. Around day 20 the warehouse also gets close to its 216-unit limit.\n- **Candidates 2, 3, 5:** they over-order a lot, so the cost of unsold units and storage clearly outweighs what they save on shortages.\n\n**Robustness:** None of these plans react to actual demand. Shortages are costly early on for everyone, since the opening stock is short until the first deliveries arrive. Batch ordering spreads demand over several days, but candidate 6 pays for that with large amounts of stock sitting in storage.\n\n**Ranking:** 0 is clearly best. The next group is 4, 7, 1 and then 6; they are close to each other, so I'm less sure about this part. Then 3, 2 and 5 are clearly worse."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 5048, "cache_read_input_tokens": 886, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 5048}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-29T07:59:17.972Z